+B3 Explainer: Monitoring and Drift  Page 1
+1. Summary
+
+The assignment's question: requests succeed and the API is available. Is the AI still behaving appropriately?
+The task was to design and test monitoring that separates "is the system up?" from "is the AI performing
+appropriately?", decide which changes matter, and say what should follow an alert.
+
+Where the hypothesis came from. The core hypothesis was submitted at the interim checkpoint:
+infrastructure health and raw feature-drift tests alone are not enough to tell whether the AI is still working
+appropriately, and feature drift should trigger investigation rather than automatic retraining. That hypothesis
+survived. What changed was our understanding of the data: the interim treated the split-half drift as a false
+alarm; later diagnosis showed it was a real model-behavior change caused by the rolling 60 s trade-intensity
+window refilling after a gap in the recording. We revised the experiment rather than forcing the original
+explanation to fit (Sections 4.3 and 5).
+
+Diagnosis.
+
+ • Serving health is good: containers broker running/healthy, mlflow running/healthy, scorer running/healthy;
+    /health reports the model loaded; 0 API errors across all scenarios; p95 latency 2.8-3.1 ms in the saved run.
+
+ • Model performance: on the reference window, PR-AUC is 0.070 against a prevalence baseline of 0.073.
+    Above-baseline performance is not established on the available data.
+
+ • The model's score correlates 0.80 with trade intensity (messages in the last 60 s) and 0.006 with the spike
+    label. Trade intensity has the model's largest weight (+0.507) but correlates -0.033 with the label.
+
+ • The supplied drift tool (Evidently, KS test) reports the harmless spread change and the alert-volume change
+    with the same kind of verdict (p close to 0) and no indication of which matters.
+
+Intervention. A tiered monitor in one script (monitoring/b3_monitor.py): Tier 1 serving status; Tier 2 drift
+graded by its effect on the model's output and alert volume; a drift severity (OK / LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH, with
+PAGE for complete serving failure); and Tier 3, a separate retrospective model-performance diagnostic.
+
+Testing. Four controlled development/evaluation scenarios, S0-S3, plus an internal threshold-robustness
+analysis.
+
