+4.3 Correction to the interim: the split-half comparison
+
+What the interim said. The interim used the split-half comparison of the reference file (first half vs second half)
+as an "unchanged" negative control. Evidently flagged 3 of 4 features, and the interim treated those flags as a
+false alarm, attributing them in part to the featurizer's 60 s trade-intensity window filling over time.
+
+What later diagnosis showed. The two halves genuinely differ. The recording has a 171 s gap, and trade
+intensity is a rolling 60 s count that refills after it. Splitting at the middle row puts the 67 pre-gap rows and 259
+refill rows into the first half, so mean trade intensity is 110 vs 303, and the model's alert rate goes from 13.5% to
+78.3% while the actual spike rate is 6.1% and 8.5%. The message rate is about the same on both sides of the
+gap, so the cause is the rolling-window refill mechanism, not a change in message rate.
+
+How the experiment was revised. The refill mechanism suggested at the interim was right; "false alarm" was
+not. The split-half comparison became S2, a real consequential change, and the no-change control was
+replaced with the interleaved odd/even split (S0), whose two halves cover the same minutes.
