+6.2 Tier 2: drift, graded by effect on the model
+
+ • Feature PSI: how far each input's distribution moved (reference deciles as bins).
+
+ • Score PSI: the same measure on the model's output. Because the score is produced by the fitted model
+    from all features together, this captures whether input changes propagate into model behavior.
+
+ • Alert-rate shift and ratio: change in the share of rows the model alerts on (operator workload).
+
+ • KS view ("before"): per-feature KS p-value flagged below 0.05, dataset drift when 50% or more features
+    are flagged (Evidently defaults). Evidently itself, run on the same pairs, gives the same counts (S0 0/4, S1
+    1/4, S2 3/4, S3 2/4).
+
+Why not the KS p-value alone? The KS p-value indicates evidence of a distributional difference under its
+assumptions but does not measure operational importance. SciPy's two-sample KS test assumes independent
+samples. Our streaming rows are serially dependent, so that assumption is not fully satisfied; we therefore do
+not treat the nominal p-values as calibrated measures of operational consequence. We retain KS as the
+baseline/before comparison and use effect size and downstream model behavior to assess consequence.
