+Does the S1 spread change move the
+model?
+
+Within these four controlled scenarios, the monitor matched the intended operational ordering: the no-change
+control stayed quiet, harmless input drift stayed low-severity, and large output/workload changes escalated.
+The KS view's dataset-level verdicts (No / No / Yes / Yes) point the same way but carry no severity or action,
+report the harmless spread change with p close to 0, catch S3 exactly at its 50% line, and say nothing about the
+model's outputs. Neither drift view nor serving health shows model performance; only Tier 3 does,
+retrospectively.
+
+10.2 Tradeoffs
+
+These are expected tradeoffs reasoned from the design; they were not measured in this project.
+
+ • Fewer drift alarms vs slower detection: grading by model impact may also quiet small real shifts; LOW
+    tickets are intended to keep them visible.
+
+ • Tier 3 lags: labels need the next 20 messages, so Tier 2 would be the earlier signal.
+
+ • Settings may need upkeep: the alert-rate thresholds are project-specific and would likely need revisiting
+    after any retrain.
+
+ • Partial serving failures: DEGRADED is reported but has no paging cutoff until a real SLO exists.
+
+ • Operating cost: Tier 1 sends real requests to the API; a busy production service would likely need to
+    sample rows.
+
+10.3 Recommendation
+
+ • Adopt the tiered monitor (Tier 1 status, drift severity ladder, separate Tier 3 diagnostic) only as a provisional,
+    limited deployment until it has been tested once on an independently recorded replay window.
+
+ • Do not retrain because of the spread drift (S1); route it to a data-quality check.
+
+ • Escalate the model for re-evaluation. Before changing the alert threshold, retraining, or replacing the
+    model, evaluate candidate alternatives on genuinely out-of-sample labeled data.
+
+ • Treat breaks and losses in the message stream as an AI risk: in S2 and S3 trade intensity changed, and so
+    did the alerts.
+
+10.4 Limitations
+
+Independent validation limitation. All supplied market-data artifacts ultimately derive from one approximately five-minute
+recording, including the repeated training data and transformed scenario files. The monitoring design was also informed by
+exploratory analysis of this recording. Therefore, S0-S3 should be interpreted as controlled development/evaluation
+scenarios rather than a held-out test set. The sensitivity analysis shows that their ordering is robust to the tested monitoring
+
+B3 Explainer: Monitoring and Drift                                        Page 16
+settings, but it does not establish generalization to unseen data or failure modes. A future evaluation should freeze the
+monitor and test it once on an independently recorded replay window.
+
+ • PAGE (UNAVAILABLE) and DEGRADED never occurred in the saved runs. They were checked only by
+    calling the status logic with constructed inputs during development (not saved as a test file), not by stopping
+    or degrading the live scorer.
+
+ • Training PR-AUC values come from re-evaluating the saved model with the training code's own split
+    (diagnosis.json).
+
+ • S3's labels are computed from the reduced message stream.
+
+10.5 Remaining uncertainty
+
+Whether the frozen monitor behaves appropriately on unseen market windows and different failure modes, and
+whether the model performs above the prevalence baseline on independent data.
+
+10.6 Team integration points
+
+These are integration points with teammates' challenges, as described in the team's materials; B3 did not
+independently verify B1 or B2 work. B2 concerns /health reporting model_loaded; B3 adds that a loaded model
+is not necessarily behaving appropriately. B1 concerns keeping up with the feed; in B3's scenarios, breaks or
+losses in the message stream changed trade intensity, which drives the model's predictions.
