+13. Likely questions
+
+Q: Why not retrain whenever drift is detected?
+    Not all drift matters. S1 has a large input shift (spread PSI 8.245) with score PSI 0.005 and almost no alert
+    change. Labeled evidence is needed to establish a performance problem, and root-cause analysis
+    determines whether retraining is appropriate.
+
+Q: Your interim said the split-half was a false alarm. Was the interim wrong?
+    The core hypothesis survived; the interpretation of that comparison did not. The refill mechanism was right,
+    but the model's alerts really changed (13.5% to 78.3%), so it was not a false alarm. We made it S2 and
+    replaced the control with the interleaved split.
+
+Q: Why PSI rather than the KS p-value?
+    The KS p-value indicates evidence of a difference under its assumptions but not its operational importance;
+    it depends on sample size, and our rows are serially dependent. PSI measures the size of a shift, and score
+    PSI and alert rate show whether the shift reaches the model's behavior. KS is kept as the before view.
+
+Q: Where do 0.10 and 0.25 come from? Did you tune them?
+    They are conventional PSI heuristic bands from credit-scorecard practice (Lewis 1994, as traced by
+    Potgieter et al. 2026), not calibrated thresholds. Only 0.10 is used for severity. The alert-rate thresholds
+    were AI-proposed project settings. Some design choices followed exploratory analysis, so we do not call the
+    experiment preregistered; instead we tested 1,485 setting combinations and the ordering did not change.
+
+Q: Is S0-S3 a test on unseen data?
+    No. All scenarios derive from one five-minute recording that also informed the design. They are controlled
+    development/evaluation scenarios; generalization to unseen data is untested.
+
+Q: Isn't S3 synthetic?
+    It is a constructed fault, built from the supplied recording and replayed through Kafka and the real featurizer.
+    S2 comes from the supplied data directly.
+
+Q: Does the model work?
+    On the available short, effectively in-sample dataset, PR-AUC does not establish performance above the
+    prevalence baseline. This does not prove the model is useless or incapable of performing better on unseen
+    data.
+
+Q: Why is the alert rate so high?
+    The model alerts on 45.9% of reference rows, mostly when trade intensity is high. The supplied logistic
+    regression uses class_weight='balanced', which assigns class weights inversely proportional to class
+    frequency during fitting. We have not tested whether this setting caused the observed alert rate.
+
+Q: What happens if the scorer fails some requests?
+    Tier 1 reports DEGRADED with the exact error count and rate; if no request produces a prediction, it is
+    UNAVAILABLE and pages. No partial-error paging cutoff was set because no SLO was supplied.
+
+B3 Explainer: Monitoring and Drift  Page 20
+Q: What would you monitor after deploying?
+
+    Tier 1 status and telemetry, score PSI and alert rate per window, Tier 3 once labels arrive, and breaks in the
+    data, since trade intensity drives the score.
+
+Q: How does B3 relate to B1 and B2?
+
+    As team integration points (not independently verified by B3): B2 concerns whether the model is loaded; B3
+    checks its behavior. B1 concerns keeping up with the feed; in B3's scenarios, breaks or losses in the
+    message stream changed trade intensity and the model's predictions.
