+B3 in plain language: a team briefing
+
+Vidhi Kothari · Challenge B3: "It Is Running, but Is It Still Working?" · For Lauren, Anastasiya and Stephen, before the
+presentation
+
+1. The question in one sentence
+
+Our API can be up, answering every request quickly, and still be giving bad answers. B3 asks: how do we tell "the
+system is up" apart from "the AI is still doing its job", and what should happen when something changes?
+The one thing to remember: a healthy service does not mean the AI is behaving appropriately. And not every
+change in the data matters. What matters is whether the change reaches the model's output and the alerts people
+receive.
+
+2. What I found (the diagnosis)
+
+• The service is healthy. In every test: 0 API errors, the model is loaded, the health check passes.
+
+• But the model's behavior is questionable. It raises an alert on 45.9% of rows, while only 7.3% are real spikes.
+    Precision at the 0.5 alert threshold is 6.4%: about 1 alert in 16 is a real spike.
+
+• Its ranking quality is near the baseline. PR-AUC is 0.070 vs a prevalence baseline of 0.073. In plain words: on
+    the data we have, we cannot show it ranks spikes better than the no-information baseline. That does not prove it
+    is useless; our data are short and effectively the same data it was trained on.
+
+• Why: the model's score mostly follows trade intensity (how many messages arrived in the last 60 seconds).
+    Score vs trade intensity: r = 0.80. Score vs actual spikes: r = 0.006, essentially no relationship.
+
+• A data quirk matters a lot. The recording has a 171-second gap. Trade intensity is a rolling 60-second count, so
+    after the gap it starts low and refills. The model sees that refill as a big change. (The message rate itself is about
+    the same on both sides: 4.92 vs 4.74 messages per second.)
+
+3. The hypothesis, and what changed since the interim
+
+The core hypothesis was already in our interim, and it survived: health checks and raw drift tests alone can't tell
+you whether the AI still works. Monitor serving health, data/prediction drift and labeled performance separately. A
+change in the input data should trigger investigation, not automatic retraining.
+
+What changed was how we read one piece of evidence. At the interim, we compared the first half of the recording
+with the second half and treated it as a "nothing changed" test, so the drift flags looked like a false alarm. Later
+measurement showed the halves really differ (because of the gap and the refill): the model's alert rate goes from
+13.5% to 78.3% while real spikes are 6.1% vs 8.5%. So that comparison became a real-change scenario (S2), and
+we built a new "nothing changed" test (S0). We revised the experiment rather than forcing the original explanation to
+fit.
+
+4. What I built (the intervention)
+
+One script, monitoring/b3_monitor.py, that checks three separate things:
+
+Layer                     What it asks                               What it says
+Tier 1: serving status    Is the service up and producing            HEALTHY / DEGRADED (some requests failed) /
+                          predictions?                               UNAVAILABLE. Only UNAVAILABLE pages
+Tier 2: drift, graded by                                             someone (PAGE).
+impact                    Did the data change, and did it reach the  OK / LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH (see below)
+Tier 3: model             model's output and the alert volume?
+performance               Once the true answers arrive, is the       A separate, after-the-fact report. It never sets the
+                          model better than the baseline?            severity.
+
+B3 team briefing                                                          Page 1
+The severity ladder in plain words: input data changed but the model didn't react = LOW (data-quality ticket). The
+model's output moved, or alerts moved moderately = MEDIUM (investigate). The alert volume is seriously disrupted
+= HIGH (escalate). The exact cutoffs (5 percentage points, 20 percentage points, 2x) are project-specific operating
+thresholds, not industry standards.
+
+5. How I tested it (the evidence)
+
+Four controlled scenarios, each comparing the original recording with a changed version. The "before" column is
+what the supplied drift tool (Evidently, using the KS test) says; the "after" column is my monitor.
+
+Scenario What it is                                         Before: features  After:   What happened to alerts
+                                                            flagged           monitor
+S0                Same data, split odd/even rows            0 of 4            OK       46.2% to 45.7%
+
+                  (nothing changed)                         1 of 4            LOW      45.9% to 46.2% (barely moved)
+
+S1                Supplied "drifted" file: wider bid/ask    3 of 4            HIGH     13.5% to 78.3%
+                                                            2 of 4            HIGH     45.9% to 1.8%, service still
+                  spread                                                               HEALTHY
+
+S2                First half vs second half (gap + refill)
+
+S3                Every other message dropped,
+
+                  replayed through Kafka
+
+The key comparison: S1 and S2 both show statistical drift. But in S1 the model's output and alert workload barely
+move, while in S2 alerts jump from 13.5% to 78.3%. That is why raw drift alone is not enough for an operational
+decision. S3 adds a second example: the service stays HEALTHY while alerts collapse from 45.9% to 1.8%.
+
+6. How much to trust it
+
+• What we know: across 1,485 tested monitoring-setting combinations, the S0-S3 ordering remained unchanged.
+    This is internal threshold robustness, not independent validation: it shows the result doesn't hinge on the
+    exact cutoffs I picked.
+
+• What we don't know: all scenarios come from one ~5-minute recording, which also shaped the design. The
+    training file is the same recording repeated. There is no independent held-out test, so we don't know how the
+    monitor or the model behave on genuinely new data.
+
+7. What I recommend
+
+• Use the monitor provisionally, in a limited way, until it is tested once on an independently recorded replay window.
+• Do not automatically retrain because input data drifted (like S1); route it to a data-quality check.
+• Re-evaluate the model on genuinely out-of-sample labeled data before changing thresholds, retraining or
+
+    replacing it.
+
+8. How B3 connects to the rest of the team
+
+These are integration points as described in the team's materials; B3 did not independently test B1 or B2 work. B2
+concerns /health reporting whether the model is loaded; B3 adds that a loaded model is not necessarily behaving
+appropriately. B1 concerns keeping up with the feed; in B3's scenarios, breaks or losses in the message stream
+changed trade intensity, which drives the model's predictions.
+
+9. My slides at a glance (about 3 minutes)
+
+Slide                                           Message                                                               Time
+                                                                                                                      ~30 s
+1. It's running. Is it still working?           Healthy service, questionable behavior; the interim hypothesis
+                                                survived                                                              ~45-50 s
+                                                                                                                      ~60-70 s
+2. Diagnosis (Figure A)                         Alerts follow trade intensity, not spikes; the split-half correction
+
+3. Intervention and evidence (Figure B) The tiers; S1 (LOW) vs S2/S3 (HIGH)
+
+B3 team briefing                                                                                                      Page 2
+4. How far to trust it  What we know / don't know / recommend  ~35-40 s
+
+Backups: full severity ladder and Tier 1 statuses; Tier 3 lift intervals (Figure C); where each setting comes from; AI
+supervision. If a question goes to one of these, hand it to me.
+
+10. Q&A: questions we might get, with short answers
+
+If you get one of these during the team Q&A, the short answer below is safe to give; for anything deeper, pass it to
+me.
+Q: Your interim called the split-half a false alarm. Was the interim wrong?
+
+   The core hypothesis survived; our interpretation of that one comparison did not. The rolling-window mechanism
+   was real, but the alerts really changed (13.5% to 78.3%), so it was not a false alarm. We made it S2 and built a
+   new no-change control (S0). The correction actually supports the hypothesis: KS flagged both S1 and S2, and
+   only the model-impact view tells them apart.
+Q: Why not just retrain whenever drift is detected?
+
+   Not all drift matters. S1 has a huge input shift but the model barely reacts. Labeled evidence is needed to establish
+   a performance problem, and root-cause analysis determines whether retraining is appropriate.
+Q: Why use PSI as well as the KS test?
+
+   The KS p-value shows evidence that distributions differ, not how much that matters. SciPy's KS test assumes
+   independent samples, and our streaming rows are serially dependent, so we don't read the p-values as calibrated.
+   PSI measures how big a shift is; applied to the model's score and alert rate, it shows whether the shift reaches the
+   model's behavior. KS stays as the "before" comparison.
+Q: Where do the PSI cut-offs 0.10 and 0.25 come from?
+
+   They are the Lewis constants, traced through Potgieter et al. (2026): conventional credit-scorecard heuristics, not
+   calibrated thresholds. Feature-engine currently defaults to 0.25 and documents 0.10 and 0.25 as commonly used
+   values. In our monitor only 0.10 affects severity; 0.25 is just a reference line.
+Q: Where do 5 pp, 20 pp and 2x come from?
+
+   They were proposed by the AI assistant and accepted by Vidhi as project-specific settings; they are not external
+   standards, and no production service-level target was supplied. That's why we tested a range of alternatives.
+Q: 1,485 out of 1,485 sounds like proof. Is it?
+
+   No. It reran the same four scenarios from the same recording with different settings. It shows the result doesn't
+   depend on the exact cutoffs; it does not show the monitor works on new data. Internal threshold robustness, not
+   independent validation.
+Q: Why is there no held-out test?
+
+   The repository has one genuine recording; the training file is that recording repeated. Re-splitting it would not give
+   independent data. The next step is to test the frozen monitor once on a newly recorded replay window.
+Q: Isn't S3 just synthetic?
+
+   S3 is pipeline-faithful: message loss was injected at the raw-message level, replayed through Kafka, and
+   recomputed by the real featurizer. But pipeline fidelity is not the same as external validity: it is still a constructed
+   fault on the same recording, not a real incident.
+Q: Why doesn't the model-performance check (Tier 3) set the severity?
+
+   The same Tier 3 outcome appears in every window, including the no-change control S0, so using it as a severity
+   would make every scenario HIGH and the monitor could no longer tell situations apart. Labels also arrive late, and
+   the intervals are exploratory.
+
+B3 team briefing                                               Page 3
+Q: What's the difference between DEGRADED and PAGE?
+
+   DEGRADED: the service is up and some predictions succeed, but some requests fail. It is reported with error
+   counts but doesn't page anyone, because no service-level target was supplied. UNAVAILABLE means no
+   predictions at all, and that pages on-call (PAGE). Neither happened in our saved runs; they were checked only
+   with constructed inputs.
+
+Q: What does "above-baseline performance not established" mean? Is the model useless?
+
+   On the available data, we do not establish PR-AUC performance above the prevalence (no-information) baseline.
+   The exploratory interval includes 0, but the rows are serially dependent and not independent out-of-sample data,
+   so we don't treat that as a formal test. This does not prove the model is useless or incapable of performing better
+   on unseen data.
+
+Q: Why does the model alert so often?
+
+   Its alerts follow trade intensity. The supplied model uses class_weight='balanced', which assigns class weights
+   inversely proportional to class frequency during fitting. We have not tested whether this setting caused the high
+   alert rate.
+
+Q: What should be tested next?
+
+   First, test the frozen monitor once on an independently recorded replay window. Second, evaluate the model on
+   genuinely out-of-sample labeled data. After that, test real DEGRADED/UNAVAILABLE serving faults and
+   investigate whether the balanced class weighting contributes to the unusually high alert rate.
+
+Q: Did AI come up with B3?
+
+   No. The hypothesis was in our interim. The AI assistant contributed specific explanations and design proposals,
+   and some were rejected after measurement, including its explanation of the split-half drift. Details are in
+   B3_AI_DECISIONS.md.
+
+Words that might come up
+
+Term                 Plain meaning
+
+Drift                The data (inputs) or the model's outputs look different from before.
+
+PSI                  A number for how much a distribution moved. Bigger = bigger shift.
+
+KS test / p-value    A statistical test of whether two samples differ; it says nothing about whether the difference
+                     matters.
+
+PR-AUC / prevalence  How well scores rank real spikes above non-spikes / the score a no-information ranking would
+                     get.
+
+Trade intensity      Number of messages in the last 60 seconds; low right after a break, then refills.
+
+Alert rate           Share of rows where the model raises a spike alert (score 0.5 or more).
+
+Full detail: B3_Explainer.pdf, B3_README.md, B3_AI_DECISIONS.md on branch B3---Vidhi.
+
+B3 team briefing                                                                                                     Page 4
+
